            <ArticleTitle>A fundamental problem of consent.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>43-6; discussion 46-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A consultant anaesthetist gave a diclofenac suppository for postoperative pain to a patient having four teeth extracted under general anaesthesia in the dental surgery. He did not seek the patient's specific consent preoperatively for use of the suppository but told her afterwards what he had done. Charged before the professional conduct committee of the General Medical Council with failure to obtain informed consent and assault, the anaesthetist was found guilty of serious professional misconduct and admonished. This decision has far reaching implications and has caused great concern.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
